  <div class="page-footer">3</div>

  <br class="page-break" style="page-break-before:always;" />

  <!-- PAGE 4 -->
  <h2 class="section-title">1.5 Significance of the Project</h2>
  <p>
    The significance of this project lies in combining community engagement with modern web technology to address inefficiencies in the local home services sector. The platform can provide a structured communication channel between household consumers, independent technicians, and administrators. This can make it easier to identify available local skills and coordinate timely domestic repairs.
  </p>
  <p>
    From a community perspective, the project promotes participation, transparency, and cooperation. Service providers are provided with an organized way to showcase their trades and secure steady work opportunities, while consumers benefit from competitive, transparent pricing and reliable service standards. Such coordination can strengthen community trust and encourage responsible use of local trades.
  </p>
  <p>
    The project is also significant from an educational perspective because it provides an opportunity to apply full-stack programming and relational database concepts to a real-world community problem. It connects technical learning with social responsibility and community-oriented problem solving.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section-title">1.6 Expected Outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    The expected outcome of the project is a functional web-based platform that can facilitate the management and coordination of local domestic service requests. The system is expected to provide separate functionalities for customers, service providers, and administrators.
  </p>
  <p>
    Through the platform, service providers should be able to register, list customized service packages, and manage appointment requests. Customers should be able to explore categorized services, locate nearby technicians via an interactive map, create bookings, and communicate with service providers in real time. Automated booking tracking and notifications should help participants monitor the progress of service appointments across pending, accepted, completed, or cancelled states.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section-title">1.7 Scope of the Report</h2>
  <p>
    This report focuses on the development and community relevance of the <strong>ServiGo Local Services Marketplace Platform</strong>. It covers the purpose and objectives of the project, background of the selected local services domain, methodology adopted for understanding the problem, community interactions and observations, analysis of the proposed solution, and the conclusions and recommendations derived from the project.
  </p>
  <div class="page-footer">4</div>

  <br class="page-break" style="page-break-before:always;" />

  <!-- PAGE 5 -->
  <h1 class="chapter-title">Chapter 2: Literature Review</h1>
  <p>
    The issue of unorganized home maintenance and domestic services has received significant attention because large numbers of households require frequent domestic interventions for electrical, plumbing, carpentry, and appliance servicing needs. Previous studies have highlighted the importance of market organization, service transparency, and digital discovery in improving the livelihoods of independent service professionals. Service marketplace initiatives encourage households and independent tradespeople to connect on structured platforms to fulfill essential community needs.
  </p>

  <div class="flowchart-container">
    <div class="flow-box">[Customer Searches Category / City] → [Views Provider Profile &amp; Map] → [Creates Booking (pending)]</div><br>
    ↓<br>
    <div class="flow-box">[Direct In-App Coordination] ← [Provider Accepts Booking (accepted)] ← [Provider Dashboard Alert]</div><br>
    ↓<br>
    <div class="flow-box">[Service Done on Site] → [Provider Marks 'Completed'] → [Customer Leaves 1-5 Star Review]</div>
  </div>
  <div class="figure-caption">Figure 2.1: End-to-End Service Booking and Fulfillment Lifecycle Flow</div>

  <table class="report-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Lifecycle Stage</th>
        <th style="width: 25%;">Triggering Actor</th>
        <th style="width: 55%;">System Action &amp; Operational Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>1. Pending</strong></td>
        <td>Customer</td>
        <td>Booking request initialized with service title, date, address, and notes.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>2. Accepted</strong></td>
        <td>Service Provider</td>
